1 NAME Daniel /Brenneman/
2 GIVN Daniel
2 SURN Brenneman
2 _MARNM Brenneman
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 10 SEP 1769
2 PLAC Darmstadt, Strkbg, Hessen ,Germany
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE MAY 1842
2 PLAC Brenneman Family Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY New Germany
3 STAE MD
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 16 MAY 1842
2 PLAC New Germany, Alleghany Co., MD
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/53899276/daniel-brenneman
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Darmstadt, Hesse, Prussia, son of Nicolaus & Barbara (Kurz) Brenneman. Married 20 Feb 1803 in Hesse, Prussia, to Maria "Mary" Bender. Died in New Germany, Allegany County, Maryland. New Germany is now in Garrett County, Ma
2 CONC ryland.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/53899276/daniel_brenneman Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 21 2024, 15:44:03 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT “Daniel D. Brenneman was born in Hessen-Darmstadt, Germany, in 1769. He married Maria Bender of Bosenhausen in 1803. They had eleven children: Jacob (who died in infancy), Jacob, Maria, Anna, Katrina, Johannes, Elizabeth, Lena, Ch
2 CONC ristian, Daniel, and Barbara. The family sailed to American and settled in Berlin, Somerset County, PA, where, as the story goes, the youngest child, Barbara, was born in a hotel. Their first farm was in what is known as “The Glad
2 CONC es” in Elk Lick Township, a lowland, perhaps even swampy during some parts of the year. Having worked hard and saved enough money, Daniel bought a farm of 100 acres just over the Mason-Dixon line near Grantsville, MD. Gerberich te
2 CONC lls Daniel’s story this way:
2 CONT With pluck and sturdy energy he proceeded to clear it and put it under cultivation. Long before his death he had done so and had also provided well for his children. Daniel Brenneman was of a retiring disposition and somewhat taci
2 CONC turn, but known as a good friend and neighbor.
2 CONT Both Daniel and Maria had been members of the Amish Mennonite church since their youth. That community of faith continued to grow near the Pennsylvania-Maryland border as other German families joined them. The National Pike (now R
2 CONC t. 40 and I-68) was bringing settlers to Pittsburgh 25 years before the first railroad was built in that area. One of the famous stops was the tavern at Little Crossing, just beside the arched bridge below Grantsville. Bender says
2 CONC  that Meshach Browning, a noted hunter and woodsman, had built a cabin and a mill there. Later, the Stone House farm was constructed with slave labor and mail service began between Cumberland and Uniontown. History records that of
2 CONC  the remaining Indians left in the area, 15 were converted to Christianity at the nearby Blooming Rose Catholic Mission and Grantsville got it’s first full time worship center, the old log Methodist Episcopal church.
2 CONT But, the Brennemans maintained their Amish Mennonite faith and convictions. They dressed in plain fashion as their ancestor had for centuries. They drove horse and buggy, did as much work as possible by hand, and rejected the “wor
2 CONC ldly” or “English” ways. Daniel Brenneman, it is said, “knew the old ways of growing flax and making linen” (Bender 4), Perhaps he was practicing his Great-great grandfather Melchoir’s trade as a weaver. Supposedly, some pieces of
2 CONC  the “Daniel Brenneman linen” still exist in that area, possibly among the Amish community.
2 CONT Daniel and Maria, having lived a happy and productive life, died before the American Civil War, in 1842 and 1856 respectively, some 14 years apart. They are buried on a hill overlooking the land that he worked and the little town 
2 CONC of New Germany, MD. The cemetery is adjacent to what are known as the “Twin Churches.” Esther Bender says, “Come from the church house door and walk straight ahead to the edge of the field. Look to the left at the fencerow of tree
2 CONC s that march across the top of the hill at the far end of the field. The tombstone is not hard to find if you simply walk to the fencerow and search on the near side of it in the field where the hill curves out of sight.”
2 CONT
2 CONT www.geocities.com/pearce2002/brenintro.doc
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Darmstadt, Hesse, Prussia, son of Nicolaus & Barbara (Kurz) Brenneman. Married 20 Feb 1803 in Hesse, Prussia, to Maria "Mary" Bender. Died in New Germany, Allegany County, Maryland. New Germany is now in Garrett County, Ma
2 CONC ryland.
2 CONT Children: (All but last (Barbara) were born in Germany before the family immigrated in 1825.)
2 CONT Jacob I Brenneman b: 27 Jul 1805 
2 CONT Jacob J Brenneman b: 28 Dec 1806 in Hessendorf, Germany 
2 CONT Mary Brenneman b: 19 Mar 1809 in Meinscheidt, Germany d.18 Jun 1891 Maple Glen Church Cem, Dorsey  Hotel Rd, Grantsville, MD 
2 CONT Anna Brenneman b: 27 Jul 1811 in Meinscheidt, Germany 
2 CONT Catherine Brenneman b: Apr 1813 in Meinscheidt, Germany (Catherine is 5th of 11 children.)
2 CONT Johannes Brenneman b: 1815 in Meinscheidt, Germany 
2 CONT Elizabeth Brenneman b: 15 Nov 1816 in Meinscheidt, Germany 
2 CONT Lena Brenneman b: 21 Apr 1819 in Meinscheidt, Germany 
2 CONT Christian B Brenneman b: 24 Jun 1821 
2 CONT Daniel D Brenneman b: 8 Jul 1824 in Meinscheidt, Germany 
2 CONT Barbara Brenneman b: 5 Apr 1827 in Berlin, Somerset, PA
