2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=38620781 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 4 2016, 21:12:24 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''From "Daniel Bender and his Descendants," compiled by Edwin and Mary Bender
2 CONT ''
2 CONT REMEMBRANCE OF GRANDPA AND GRANDMA BENDER
2 CONT By Fannie Swartzenruber Miller Martin
2 CONT
2 CONT (The following was written for the 1964 Bender Reunion which was held at the Bender Homestead near Goshen, Indiana.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa, Daniel Bender, was born near Tavistock, Ontario, Canada, April 28, 1835, where he grew up with seven brothers and sisters; namely, John, Chris, Marie, Samuel, Jacob, David, and Barbara, he being second to the last.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1858, he was married to Veronica Roth who was born February 23, 1839. She was a lovely little French lady who came to Canada by boat when eight years old. The trip was made in six weeks. I had the pleasure of visiting the farm 
2 CONC where Grandpa and Grandma began housekeeping. In fact, I enjoyed having supper with the Benders that still own the farm, or did in 1948.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple decided to move to the United States in 1859 or 1860. They boarded the train in Tavistock to seek their fortune. On this trip a very unusual thing happened. Perhaps not many remember this: a swindler also seeking his fo
2 CONC rtune somehow robbed Grandpa of the little money they had saved, amounting to $100.00. Arriving in Goshen penniless was certainly a sad story.
2 CONT
2 CONT They bought a farm of 160 acres six miles east of Goshen for $1.25 an acre. It changed hands only twice; first to Uncle Noah in 1912, then to Uriah Bontragers in 1942. (In 1966 the farm land was sold to Herbert Yoder, a son-in-law
2 CONC  of Orus Benders.) On the part of Grandpa this was pretty tough going. I remember when I returned for a visit, the first thing Grandma said was, "Fanny, we are out of debt." They built most of the buildings which took many years t
2 CONC o accomplish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa was often confronted with other people's problems and gave advice in business transactions. He was a farmer and butcher. As a beekeeper, he would often get his big straw hat covered with netting and shake the bees from a l
2 CONC imb. He would say, "We need the queen first, then the other bees will follow." Usually we kept our distance, and ran if necessary.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa and Grandma enjoyed many travels int heir days. In 1892, when I was two years old, we made a trip to Canada and several trips to Iowa ... There were many other short trips, most enjoyable for Grandpas, to Brown County to v
2 CONC isit friends ... Aunt Kate and Grandpa took a trip to Missouri to visit her parents. I kept the home fires burning. So, all in all, with the bad start, things turned out real well.
2 CONT
2 CONT How I thank my grandparents for a wonderful heritage. I was taught the Holy Scriptures. Now in this age, many seem to ignore these teachings. Resist this and always do good int he name of Christ.
2 CONT
2 CONT God bless everyone as they are here today enjoying this get-to-gether to renew old acquaintances, and getting acquainted with the new. Written in love and remembrance of our dear grandparents, uncles, and aunts.
2 CONT Son of Jacob Bender and Magdalena Brenneman.  Husband of Veronica "Fannie" Roth.
2 CONT Father of Mattie Miller, Christian, Anna Swartzendruber ,Mary Miller, Noah, Daniel, Menno, Ezra, and Lydia Bender (1880-1904).
2 CONT Member of the Old Order Amish Mennonite Church.
2 CONT ____________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT "1835 the 28th of April was born my 8th son and is named Daniel.  The Lord be with and by him as long as he lives"
2 CONT (Translated German inscription in Jacob Bender Bible located in the Mennonite Historical Society Library at Goshen, IN)-Transcribed from German script by Nelson P. Springer (1977) and translated by Lorraine Roth (1986).* Reference
