1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH: MILLER. - Gideon D. Miller was born Sept. 22, 1862; died April 6, 1941; aged 78 y. 6 m. 15 d. He suffered a light paralytic stroke on Saturday evening, March 29, and remained in a critical condition all week. He is survived
2 CONC  by 3 sons (Dwight, Myron and Aaron) and 8 grandchildren, all of Springs, Pa. He was one of a family of 12 children, 8 of whom survive him, as follows: William of Salem, Ohio; Ulysses of Meyersdale, Pa.; Malinda, wife of F. W. Ben
2 CONC der, Springs, Pa.; Anna L. of Springs; Edward of Inwood, W. Va.; Clarence of Elizabeth, Pa.; Minnie, wife of Fred Lohr, Lancaster, Pa.; and Cora, wife of Charles Shoemaker, Scottdale, Pa. One brother and 2 sisters preceded him in 
2 CONC death (Milton; Lydia, wife of J. H. Bender, and Sally, wife of D. H. Bender). On Feb. 15, 1891 he was married to Katy Otto, who passed away Nov. 5, 1940. He united with the Mennonite Church June 17, 1888. He was ordained to the mi
2 CONC nistry Nov. 13, 1891, in which capacity he served faithfully until his health did not permit, a little more than two years ago. As a man of conviction, and level-headed in counsel, his voice had great weight in the counsel of the 
2 CONC Church. Funeral services were held April 9 in the Springs Mennonite Church in charge of S. G. Shetler of Johnstown, Pa., assisted by Bro. Roy Kinsinger of Grantsville, Md., and Bro. Roy Otto of Springs. The remains were laid to re
2 CONC st in the cemetery near the Springs Church. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A Family of Inventors
2 CONT All of Dan's sons were of a creative, inventive turn, most of them possessing mechanical genius. Two of the main inventions of the oldest son, Gideon, were a traction engine and a garden tractor. Another son, Milton, built the fir
2 CONC st automobile of the area. Both Ed and Clarence had a goodly measure of inventive genius and turned out a variety of useful articles in the Miller shops. Ulysses (Uli) expressed his genius through music and became the leading viol
2 CONC inist and violin teacher of the area. When Ed was a young man he had intentions to educate himself to become a teacher. He desired the highest education obtainable. The Sunday following this decision his brother Milt visited home 
2 CONC for the purpose of persuading Ed and his family that the young man should stay in his father's shop and become a mechanic. Having heard this Ed intended to go without dinner and started out through the orchard. But Milt saw him an
2 CONC d followed. In the discussion that followed, Milt won.
2 CONT Later on Ed had a letter from his father, Dan, who was troubled with sleepless nights because he had persuaded
2 CONT instead of letting Ed decide for himself. He told a friend he wished he had two sons-one to attend school and become a teacher, one to take over his shops.
2 CONT Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000218859898252label=@S45@
